Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A tax on immutable and observable traits would raise revenue without distorting incentives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because a tax changes behaviour only where the taxed thing can be changed, a levy on fixed and visible characteristics such as height would raise revenue with no deadweight loss, which is what a consistent optimal-tax theory recommends.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The proposal is a reductio rather than a policy: nobody supports it, and the reason nobody supports it is evidence that our tax intuitions are not in fact grounded in welfare maximisation.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mankiw uses the argument this way, as an internal critique. The force of it depends on the traits genuinely correlating with earning capacity, which the height literature does report, and on there being no independent objection to taxing people for what they are rather than for what they do.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utility cannot be compared across individuals\">Utility cannot be compared across individuals</a><ul><li>The other half of the internal critique of utilitarian taxation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same\">Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same</a><ul><li>The incidence result this argument relies on.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation\">The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation</a><ul><li>The same non-distortion logic applied to an immobile factor rather than a person.</li></ul></li></ul>","China’s defeat in the Sino-Japanese war undermined Chinese belief in their cultural superiority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The reason for end of Chinese belief in their cultural superiority can be traced back to the defeat of China during the Sino-Japanese War. This marked the ultimate turning point after a series of defeats at the hands of the West.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a turning point in Chinese history when the prevailing belief of Chinese cultural superiority was shattered.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Prior to coming into contact with the West in an extended degree, all the peoples that lived around China were heavily influenced by Chinese culture and politically and militarily less than or at the same level as the Chinese. Even when this was not true, Chinese culture still often drastically influenced them. With the defeat during the Opium Wars, wars with other European powers such as Russia and France and finally the Japanese, the Chinese ultimately had to realize the possibility of Western culture being something that is valuable to study and adopt elements from. Prior to the Sino-Japanese War, they had only considered Western technology to be superior, not their culture.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a><ul><li>Japan ultimately embraced the influence of the West before China did, however.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>This seems to be one thing that was changed by Western culture, which had a larger focus on positive science.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion\">When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion</a><ul><li>China was ultimately unable to reform, and in the resulting revolution, many people argued for the adoption of many aspects of Western values, such as constitutional liberalism and democracy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucius reinterpreted existing moral teachings":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>By the time of Confucius, the core classic works that existed in China were already well-established, so Confucius essentially served as a reinterpreter and synthesizer of existing moral ideas, bringing them together into a cohesive moral framework.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Confucius did not invented a wholly new moral framework, but rather reshaped the existing moral fabric.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Based on the existing texts, Confucius gave new interpretations and functioned as perhaps the first teacher in Chinese history who spread his teachings across a great geographic extent. Therefore, his teachings became widespread and influential.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>This could make sense since Confucius was China’s “first great teacher,” so they would focus heavily on education.</li></ul></li></ul>","Contemplation is the highest form of happiness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Of all the different things that bring about happiness, contemplation is the one that brings about the most profound/highest form of happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core aspect of the happy and virtuous life.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Happiness is achieved in its highest form with contemplation since:</p><ul><li>Contemplation is the highest, and most continuous of all activities. We are most capable of practicing it, and we can do it anywhere, at anything.</li><li>Contemplation is the most pleasurable thing. Those with knowledge are more pleasured than those still searching for knowledge.</li><li>Contemplation is self-sufficient, and it is something we can do by ourselves.</li><li>Contemplation is appreciated for its own sake. It is not a means to an end; it is the end.</li><li>Contemplation must be the activity of the gods (if only that is because they have nothing else to do).</li></ul><p>Moral activity is secondary happiness, since it engages with worldly and practical things, whereas contemplation is the greatest form of happiness, since it concerns the heavenly/divine.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>At its highest, this means contemplation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a><ul><li>But contemplation is not enough, because one has to first be physically well-off.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>This, although it is important, is not as important as contemplation when it comes to achieving happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As history advances, humans have become more rationalistic, and thus, the religious motive is abandoned in the spirit of capitalism, and religious compulsion is no longer needed to justify economic acquisition.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Weber’s final step in his argue for the development of the spirit of capitalism, as once the religious element fades away, all that is left is the modern conception of capitalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>The idea that gets detached.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Once the calling is gone, traditionalism loses its religious opponent.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","Economic tax incidence is based on relative price elasticity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Who the tax actually affects is the product of relative price elasticities between the consumers and producers. The side that is more inelastic bears more of the tax.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the mechanism that economic tax incidence operates by.</p><h4>Diagram</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For regulators, taxes are generally more effective tools than bans\">For regulators, taxes are generally more effective tools than bans</a><ul><li>This note points out something that regulators should consider.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system\">Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system</a><ul><li>Perhaps tax can be rebated from the side that benefited more to the side that benefitted less?</li></ul></li></ul>","Ethics is grounded in religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That ethics is fundamentally the result of religion, or that the “true” ethics is at least the result of religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the argument made by many religious authorities in order to present religion as something that gives some fundamental order to the world.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It is argued by some that our currently set of moralities is the byproduct of Christianity, such that even when we push back against Christianity today, we are in fact using a Christian set of morality in order to make such an argument.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>But this doesn’t seem to be the nature of the current moral landscape.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>Essentially the argument made here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>Similar to the argument made before.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A major claim of the Austrian schools is that fiat money derives its value from the fact that it has historically been backed up by commodities. If that were never to have occurred for some form of fiat money, then it would have failed to continue to function.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Austrian understanding of the nature of fiat money.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>This seems a little confusing to me, since the last time the US was on the gold reserve was in 1971, and it has since been 50 years since cash was exchangeable for gold. Yet, the currency continues to function. Something like 77% of the current US population was born after the end of the gold standard, and yet the fiat currency continues to function. It just seems implausible for fiat money to merely be a continuation of commodity-backed money.</p><h4>References</h4>","Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle rejects the idea of a uniform “goodness.” He argues that goodness is a heterogenous concept whereby there is not a common characteristic corresponding to one idea.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>With this idea, Aristotle grounds ethics. Goodness is no longer some abstract Idea or Form. Rather, it is something actionable.</p><h4>Argument</h4><p>We first must separate the idea of goodness into things that are <em>good in an of themselves,</em> and things that are <em>good as a means to secure something that is good in an of itself</em>. Here, only the former is examined, since the second sense of goodness is not the sense that we ought to strive for in life, i.e., the second sense of goodness is contingent and lower in importance. With respect to the first kind of good then, if there is one overarching Platonic Idea of “goodness” that underly all concepts we consider good (e.g., honor, intelligence, etc.), then there must be some kind of similarity between these in how they all seem to approach the same concept of “goodness.” Yet this does not seem to be the case. Therefore, we must conclude that goodness is different for each of its incarnations, and all of its incarnations do not approach on some central, underlying concept of “goodness.” Rather, goodness is something that changes and adapts to the specific concepts that it is currently being applied to.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>In this section of the text (specifically, 1096b26) Aristotle raise the question of for what reason, if any, can all these different concepts be called “good” given that they do not all approach on a central concept of “goodness.” He does not address this issue explicitly in the rest of his philosophy. However, Aristotle does define a broad conception of goodness: “an activity of the soul that is in accordance with virtue.” This could be put forth as a solution to the problem presented above, as while it is not a strong, Platonic definition of good, it does bring a sense of unity to the concept of goodness. His argument for such a definition is as follows:</p><ol><li>The function of a person is an activity of the soul in accordance with a rational principle.</li><li>A good human is a rational human.</li><li>Something done well is done according to its excellence.</li></ol><p>$\\therefore$ What is good is an activity that is in accordance with virtue.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a></li></ul>","Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>in most cases, the government should not interfere in the affairs of private citizens, since such intervention is either inefficient or actively harmful.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core ideas in Mill’s <em>On Liberty</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Government intervention is not justified for the following reasons:</p><ol><li>Individuals can do tasks better without government intervention.</li><li>It is good for individuals to do something, even though the government may do it ore efficiently, because it helps to develop the citizenry.</li><li>It unnecessary to add to the powers of government, which can be abused.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>Another reason why government should not intervene.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a><ul><li>When intervention occurs, it has negative consequences.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the tolerant nature of Hinduism, which offers many “different paths to the same top of the hill,” religion conflict is reduced in India.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrate how particular viewpoints towards the religion, more specifically, the acceptance towards religious values different than yours, may shape a civilization in ways beyond the immediate influence of the religion itself.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hinduism is very tolerant of other religions, leading to very little religious conflict in India throughout history, except when outside religions attempted to move in (usually headed by a hostile army).</p>","Humans are the most ‘political’ animal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Humans are the most political of all the different species of animals. Here, political means having a single/common goal in mind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason for the writing of the books.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Humans are the type of animal who are best able to cooperate in order to achieve some grand goal. Additionally, humans have the ability for speech, which fosters this politicality, and they have a sense of morality, which also strengthens this feeling.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics\">Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics</a><ul><li>This is one elaboration on humans’ political nature.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>Justice in politics according to Aristotle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences\">Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences</a><ul><li>The practice of politics is complicated and involves various parts.</li></ul></li></ul>","Instinct outperforms intellect under extreme conditions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Reasoning is a slow instrument that requires information and time, and in conditions that supply neither, an inherited disposition that encodes no reasons at all will outperform it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This bounds the claim that rational deliberation is the superior guide to action, and locates the boundary at the point where the cost of being wrong once is total.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The contrast in <em>To Build A Fire</em> is between a man who can calculate temperatures and a dog that cannot, and only one of them survives. The general form is that tacit knowledge is robust where explicit knowledge is brittle.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love\">Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love</a><ul><li>A parallel limit on the authority of reason, in a different domain.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>The same superiority of dispersed and tacit knowledge, at the level of a system.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should strive to overcome emotion with reason\">One should strive to overcome emotion with reason</a><ul><li>The counsel this claim qualifies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Insurance companies are not the main reason for the high costs in the US health care system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Health care insurance providers are not the main cause of the high prices of US health insurance. Rather, the high cost is due to a combination of the providers and the AMA.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides an alternative argument for the reason that health care costs are so high in the US.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several things which point to the fact that the reason for the high cost of health care is not the insurance companies:</p><ul><li>The profits of insurance companies are low compared to the average in the S&amp;P 500, with the largest insurance company having a profit margin of 6%, and the second largest having between 2 and 4%. The average profit margin of a company on the S&amp;P 500 is around double that.<ul><li>The paltry margin means that if the largest insurance company, UnitedHealth Group, were to donate every single dollar of its profits to buying Americans more health care, they would only be able to buy 9% more.</li><li>Even if we were to look at the return on equity, the ROE of health insurance companies are still lower than that of the general population of companies in the S&amp;P 500. Regardless, the financial success of these companies don’t matter, since neither way their small profit margins means that they can’t pay out that much extra health insurance anyways.</li></ul></li><li>The majority of the cost comes from the higher cost by providers.<ul><li>The doctors and hospitals that actually provide care at the largest driver of health care costs, which demand some government intervention in order to negotiate the lower cost of drugs (through if done poorly, this may reduce innovation).</li><li>Due to the litigious nature of the US health care system, doctors will practice defensive medicine even in ambiguous situation. </li></ul></li></ul><h4>Addendum</h4><p>Americans actually pay a smaller amount out-of-pocket compared to many other rich countries. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized\">The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized</a><ul><li>Even though their system is highly socialized, it seems that the Japanese pay more out of pocket compared to Americans.</li></ul></li><li><em>How to Cure Health Care</em><ul><li>Some of the points made here are also made in the linked article.</li></ul></li></ul>","LLM hallucinations are 'shameless guesses,' not 'hallucinations'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that LLM \"hallucinations\" are not actually hallucinations in the way that we think about a hallucination. Rather, it is just a guess the LLM made.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that we have to rethink how we conceptualize mistakes that AI make. This has implications for how we think about the nature of AI agents as being bullshit machines.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The common perception of “hallucinations” is that is a form of <em>sui generis</em> mistake that AIs make which is entirely foreign to humans.</li><li>Another way to view this, however, is to see hallucinations as probably guesses that the AI makes because its utility function does not take into account “shame” that someone might feel if they made a wild guess.</li><li>This lack of shame is due to the nature of the training process, where the AI is rewarded for correct guesses, but it is not punished for wrong ones.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Material incentives are only part of the reason that people may produce new ideas. Other large justifications for the production of new ideas include scientific curiosity, as well as a general desire to benefit humanity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common argument when attempting to point out flaws in the patent system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>I think that this is true to a certain degree. However, I think that this is more so true in the past than today. In the past, often those who were able to conduct high-level/cutting edge scientific research were singular, wealthy people who had time on their hands. Today, not only do new scientific breakthrough take more equipment, they also often require large teams of people working in tandem. The high cost of equipment and the difficulty of uniting the motivations of many people (who are working for a wage, not for leisure) means that financial incentives would far outweigh other incentives in the academic environment today.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one\">The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one</a></li></ul>","Positive sodium ions that spread through passive diffusion causes a signal to be transported through a neuron":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, the sodium outside the cell (which is positive and existing in the higher concentration gradient), floods into the cell, causing depolarization. Through this process, they passively diffuse around the point of entry, causing nearby areas to reach the threshold potential, thereby causing depolarization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the crucial process by which a signal is transported down a neuron.</p><h4>References</h4>","Social Security is a progressive system where higher income earners receive a lower share of their lifetime earnings":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Social Security paid for by payroll taxes, though only the first $168,600 of income is taxed in this manner. However, high income earners also receive a lower share of their total lifetime earnings in terms of their benefits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of the Social Security system.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>The fiscal counterpart. Progressivity concerns who bears the cost; that note concerns whether the programme is solvent at all.</li></ul></li></ul>","Socialism has a knowledge problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The biggest problem that Hayek identified for the socialist economy is the fact that the central planners lack the sufficient knowledge to be able to allocate resources in an economy effectively.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This both differs from the economic calculation problem that is presented by Mises, but also represents an attack of socialism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Fundamentally, the way this argument differs from the economic calculation problem is that this only states that there is an epistemic problem. There is an unknowableness of the necessary information in order to run command economies in command economies. It does not state that such information does not exist in command economies. Specifically, Hayek refers to the “knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place” as the information that the central planner is fundamentally missing. Since circumstances for a specific individual are constantly changing, it is in fact impossible for the central planner to aggregate all this information.</p><ul><li>Part of this is Polanyi knowledge, which is knowledge that is intangible and unable to be quantized in any way.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“A simple model of AI-aided economic growth”</p><p>The Solow model has its uses, but it fails when it comes to major changes stemming from AI.  Consider instead an economy with (at least) two factors of production:</p><blockquote><ol><li><strong>Intelligence</strong>.  Yes, formal smarts.  Playing chess, proving math theorems, and doing well on evals.  Don’t forget humans can do those things too, though AIs are now a huge boost here.</li><li><strong>Polanyi knowledge</strong>.  Michael Polanyi, that is.  This refers to knowledge of time and place, inarticulable knowledge, custom and habit, and many other particularities that you can read about in Hayek and Polanyi and in many other social scientists, anthropologists too.</li></ol></blockquote></blockquote><p>What’s more, by the very nature of aggregated information, the official statistics, minute variations are smoothed over. Yet it is specifically these specific pieces of information that is valuable in the first place.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>This presents the economic calculation problem.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI represents a new epistemological category\">AI represents a new epistemological category</a><ul><li>It seems that even this new epistemological category might struggle with Polanyi knowledge though?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation\">Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Systems analysis works best when the system is static":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that systems analysis is best applicable and most accurate when the system is not dynamic and unpredictable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines when systems analysis ought not to be used.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Systems analysis does not work very well when the system is dynamic is changes drastically (and often unpredictably) with a slight change in input.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>When scientists (lead by Song Jian) attempted to use systems theory to model birth rates during the formation of the one-child policy, th ey failed, and China now suffers extremely low birth rates</li><li>When Jay Forrester attempted to model the entire world through the flow of capital and resources, he was not able to predict the future accurately either.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>It is this tendency that makes systems complex. They \"fight back.\"</li></ul></li></ul>","The Efficient Market Hypothesis cannot account for market anomalies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH) is not able to account for market anomalies such as the Momentum Effect or the January Effect.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates that there are moments when the market becomes inaccurate predictors of all information relating to an asset.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Market anomalies exist for three main reaons:</p><ol><li>Limits in risk models: if there are inaccuracies with the basic models of the EMH, then what may actually be risk hedging could appear as if they were anomalies.</li><li>Arbitrage is not always risk-free and frictionless.</li><li>Humans are not perfectly rational and they may make mistakes or suffer data issues.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a><ul><li>What markets do when they are well structured.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Financial momentum is predictive of the outcome of a prediction market\">Financial momentum is predictive of the outcome of a prediction market</a><ul><li>An anomaly that disobeys the EMH.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutional investors ensure that individual firm are correctly priced\">Institutional investors ensure that individual firm are correctly priced</a><ul><li>Helps provide price information in markets.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Imperial Chinese state’s monopoly on knowledge prevented a scientific revolution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The reason for the lack of a Chinese scientific revolution is the fact that China held a strict monopoly on scientific innovation which prevented the advancement of science that would have kicked-started such a revolution.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason for the lack of a scientific revolution in China despite the high levels of scientific knowledge that they had and should have been able to accrue.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The study of science was first and foremost largely reduced to a predictive endeavor, since the political nature of science meant that there was no reason for the state to adopt a causal-theoretic model. What’s more, the individuals that could afford to learn about science on their own were the scholar-officials with deep ties to the state, which means that they have a vested interest in not letting knowledge to the public which may damage the regime. Astronomy, for instance, was something that was banned to be studied by the general public.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>Once they are integrated, they do not want science to be able to de-stabilize the regime and lead to the loss of their power.</li></ul></li></ul>","The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US has a marginal tax system, meaning that for each additional dollar of income earned, that dollar is taxed at a higher rate. In essence, this means that the US has a progressive taxation policy. The US does not have a flat tax system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important to understand, as many people misunderstand how the US tax system works, believing it to be a flat tax rate. This is important to understand when analyzing the US tax system.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a></li></ul>","The difference principle expresses fraternity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle expresses a sense of fraternity among the people who participate in it, which strengthens the social glue that holds everybody together.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the advantage that the difference principle offers.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the principle expresses a certain sense of concern for the less well off, similar to the way the concern is expressed in the family (where fraternity is most present), it can be said to generalize fraternity to the societal level.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>Another argument for the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often claimed that the high taxes of the 1950s was what enabled the explosive growth during the period. However, this is in fact not true. While on paper, the top marginal tax rate was around 91%, the actual effective tax rate was much higher.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This dispels a common misconception that there were incredibly high tax rates during the 1950s.</p><h4>Rent-seeking behavior</h4><ul><li>People began to pay for luxury goods with company funds, since it was a fully deductible expense for the company and tax-free for the individual. This also created a culture whereby business decisions were influenced by their tax-deductibility rather than their economic merit.</li><li>Executives began to be paid with stock options, since the capital gains tax rate is far lower than the income tax rate.</li><li>Corporations were created simply as a way of manipulating the tax system, so that people pay the lower capital gains tax, rather than the far higher income tax rate.</li><li>Lobbying commenced in an attempt to reduce the tax rate of specific groups, most infamously, the Oil Depletion Allowance, where oil men were allowed to deduct 27.5% of their gross income from taxes.</li></ul><h4>Effective Tax Rate</h4><ul><li>The top 0.1% of individuals paid, on average during that period, an effective federal, state, and local tax rate of ~52%.</li><li>The top 1% of individuals paid, on average during that period, an effective federal, state, and local tax rate of ~52%.</li></ul><p> </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>Something that is needed to understand the US tax system and this note specifically.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes\">An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes</a><ul><li>Related to the idea of tax rates, which is extensively discussed by this note. It could be the case that if an unrealized gains tax were to be implemented, there would be even more rent-seeking behavior.</li></ul></li></ul>","The minimum wage can reduce unemployment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While traditional economic logic suggests that having a minimum wage reduces unemployment because some people whose productivity are below the minimum won’t be hired, theoretically, the claim could be made that minimum wage in fact increase employment.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the view of the traditional economist and traditional economic logic, which suggests that the minimum wage actually raises employment rather than reduce it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Say there are two people, John and Jack, the former ready to accept a wage of $10 and the latter $15. Assuming that the firm can hire only at a standard wage, and the revenue per worker is $16.</p><ol><li>In a case without minimum wage, the firm would only hire John, since they make a profit of $6 at a wage for John of $10. </li><li>In a case where the minimum wage is $15, the firm would rationally hire both John and Jack, since at that point, adding an additional worker only increases their revenue, not decrease it. I.e., hiring just John means that the firm would make $1 of profit, while hiring both John and Jack means that the firm would make $2 of profit.</li></ol><p>Therefore, there is a situation whereby having a minimum wage actually increases employment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This is a potential refutation against the idea that deregulation is always beneficial for the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","There exist eternal and perfect metaphysical frameworks of physical things":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is a common idea in idealistic schools of philosophy, such as the Platonic school or the Neo-Confucianists.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This often represents a crucial aspect of the metaphysics of the aforementioned schools.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>Neo-Confucianism (School of Principle)</h5><p><em>Li</em> (理) is the thing which exists within all things in the world, and is what makes that thing <em>it</em>, as opposed to something else. It can be conceived of as being the form of the object. There is no thing without <em>li</em>. In the physical world, objects manifest as the combination of <em>qi</em> (气) and <em>li</em>. Things come into existence through the <em>qi</em> condensing in accordance with the <em>li</em> and therefore makes the object part of a category of objects as a whole, and disappear through the <em>qi</em> decondensing. <em>Li</em> is conceived of as being ontologically/explanatorily prior to the <em>qi</em>. <em>Li</em> in the most universal/comprehensive level manifests as the highest (ultimate) <em>li</em>, is termed the Supreme Ultimate (太极). The Supreme Ultimate is the ultimate pattern according to which the dynamic processes of <em>qi</em> (e.g., ying-yang, activity-stillness) become intelligible. <strong>Comparison with Aristotle</strong> A comparison can be drawn here with Aristotle’s four causes:</p><ul><li><em>Material</em>: is akin to the <em>qi</em>.</li><li><em>Formal</em>: is akin to the <em>li</em>.</li><li><em>Efficient</em>: is very roughly equivalent to the transformations/interactions of the <em>qi</em>.</li><li><em>Final</em>: no clean equivalent in the Neo-Confucianist framework, though some aspects of the <em>li</em> can embody such a normative/teleological sense.</li></ul><h4>Addendum</h4><p>It is important to note here that there is scholarly debate regarding the precise nature of Zhu Xi’s metaphysics, with some calling him a type of dualist, and others disputing that label. It is difficult to say how exactly the <em>li</em> is separable from the <em>qi</em>.</p><h4>References</h4>","Utility cannot be compared across individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is no common scale on which one person’s gain in welfare can be measured against another’s loss, which means the utilitarian instruction to maximise aggregate welfare has no determinate content.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the technical objection that removes the foundation of welfare-maximising redistribution, independently of any moral objection to it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pareto improvement does not take into account the relative strength of utilities or disutilities\">Pareto improvement does not take into account the relative strength of utilities or disutilities</a><ul><li>The same limitation, expressed as a defect in the standard efficiency criterion.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>The broader conclusion this supports.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>An alternative criterion that avoids the comparison.</li></ul></li></ul>","pH is determined by the concentration of H+ atoms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>How basic (alkaline) or acidic a solution is determined by the concentration of $H^+$ atoms in the solution. Higher concentration indicates alkalinity, and lower concentration indicates acidity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for AP Bio, as Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds.</p><h4>Formula</h4><p>$$pH = -\\log[H^+]$$</p><h4>The pH Scale</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds\">Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds</a></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Pasted image 20250826093301.png</span></li></ul>"});
